course: dictionary
lesson: 16137
title: Wholeheartedly: with all your heart
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wholeheartedly is an adverb. It tells you how someone does something: with all their heart, completely and without any doubt. Look for the word heart inside it, and you already have part of the meaning.
explain: «I agree wholeheartedly.» The speaker is not just a little bit in favour. They say yes completely and sincerely, with no doubt at all. To check, swap in "completely and sincerely": if it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
explain: In the story, Badger, Rat and Mole help Toad wholeheartedly once he changes his ways. They give all their help, because they truly want Toad to do well. The words around the word show you how much they mean it.
explain: At school, «throw yourself into it wholeheartedly» means give the task your full effort. Use wholeheartedly when you want to show that you really mean something, with no holding back.
fact: Wholeheartedly is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done.
fact: Wholeheartedly means with all your heart: completely and sincerely, without any doubt.
fact: «I agree wholeheartedly» means you agree completely, with no doubt at all.
fact: The word is built from whole, heart and -ly: whole-hearted, then -ly makes it an adverb.
fact: Its opposite idea is half-heartedly, which means with only some of your effort or interest.
exercise: «I agree wholeheartedly.» What does wholeheartedly mean here? | Sometimes, but not always | *Completely and sincerely | Only a little bit | Loudly and angrily
exercise: «They supported the plan wholeheartedly.» Which phrase means the same as wholeheartedly? | Just in case it failed | Quietly, in the back room | *With all their heart | With some doubts and hesitation
exercise: «Ben throws himself into his science project wholeheartedly.» What does this tell you about Ben? | *He gives it all his effort and enthusiasm | He does not care about it | He does it only because he has to | He stopped halfway through
test: «Mum welcomed our new neighbours wholeheartedly.» How did Mum welcome them? | Without speaking to them | Rudely and coldly | *Warmly, with all her heart | Only for a minute
test: Which sentence uses wholeheartedly correctly? | *«Maya joined the team wholeheartedly and practised every day.» | «Maya wholeheartedly ate her lunch in a hurry.» | «Maya was wholeheartedly tall for her age.» | «Maya joined the team wholeheartedly, then left the next day with no reason.»
exam: Which word is an adverb that means completely and sincerely, with no doubt at all? | Heartbroken | *Wholeheartedly | Halfway | Wholehearted
